<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!-- Now goes through androidx.core.splashscreen's compat API instead
         of relying solely on the raw platform SplashScreen attributes —
         see MainActivity.kt's onCreate() for the installSplashScreen()
         call, and build.gradle's core-splashscreen dependency comment,
         for the full reasoning: the raw platform attributes (still set
         below, unchanged — Theme.SplashScreen reads the same
         windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon etc. attribute names) were
         confirmed correct and byte-identical to Echo Nightly's own
         working values/themes.xml, but silently fell back to the bare
         static launcher icon specifically on Realme UI 6.0 (Android 15)
         while working on other devices. The compat library sits between
         the theme and the platform API and can force a manually-drawn
         fallback when the platform doesn't cooperate, giving the
         animated icon a second path to actually render.

         parent changed from a plain framework theme to Theme.SplashScreen
         (required by the compat library — it looks for specific
         attributes that only exist in its own theme hierarchy) — this
         theme is ONLY ever active for the splash-screen instant itself
         (see postSplashScreenTheme below, which is what the Activity
         switches to immediately after), so this does not change the
         rest of the app's look, unlike changing NormalTheme's parent
         would have.

         REMOVED android:windowBackground from this theme (was
         @drawable/launch_background) — this is the confirmed actual
         root cause of the static-launcher-icon fallback surviving even
         after adding the compat library. Android's own SplashScreen
         migration docs state this explicitly: "If your existing splash
         screen is implemented using a custom theme that overrides
         android:windowBackground, the system replaces your custom
         splash screen with a default Android system splash screen" —
         that "default Android system splash screen" IS the bare
         launcher icon on its adaptive-icon card, i.e. exactly the
         screenshot this was fixing. LaunchTheme was carrying this
         attribute over from before the SplashScreen migration (the
         pre-Android-12 pattern, where a themed windowBackground WAS the
         whole splash mechanism) — it was never removed when
         windowSplashScreenBackground was added alongside it, so the
         theme was simultaneously using both the legacy AND the new
         splash mechanisms, which is exactly the documented trigger for
         Android discarding the custom one. Confirmed Echo Nightly's own
         working splash theme never sets android:windowBackground at
         all. windowSplashScreenBackground below is sufficient on its
         own for the splash's background color — a plain android:
         windowBackground is only relevant to NormalTheme, for the
         Activity's post-splash background (see NormalTheme below, and
         MainActivity.kt's gray-flash-fix comment for why that one
         still needs it). -->
    <style name="LaunchTheme" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/bgColor</item>
        <!-- Animation intentionally static, duration 0: the animated
             intro is now owned entirely by Flutter's SplashScreen
             widget (lib/screens/splash_screen.dart), not by this native
             layer. Keeping any animation here (art_splash_anim, 900ms)
             would play BEFORE Flutter's first frame, and the Dart
             splash would then play AFTER it as a second, unrelated
             animation — exactly the "native splash finishes, then a
             visibly different animation restarts" discontinuity this
             project has already hit once before (see main.dart's
             SplashScreen wiring comment). windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon
             still needs a value (the platform requires one), so it
             points at the plain static launcher icon with
             animationDuration=0 — effectively invisible, since
             Flutter's first frame (with SplashScreen's overlay already
             painting) takes over on the very next frame regardless. -->
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@mipmap/ic_launcher</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimationDuration">0</item>
        <!-- FIX (icon rendering oversized): "icon_preferred" tells the
             platform to render the launcher's adaptive-icon layers at
             full bleed instead of masking them down to the platform's
             standard small centered splash-icon size — that full-bleed
             behavior is exactly why the icon filled so much of the
             screen. Removing this line reverts to the platform default
             (icon_default), which applies the normal small, centered,
             masked splash icon sizing/padding used by every other app's
             cold-start splash. -->
        <!-- <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBehavior" tools:targetApi="TIRAMISU">icon_preferred</item> -->
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/NormalTheme</item>
    </style>
    <style name="NormalTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.Black.NoTitleBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/bgColor</item>
    </style>
</resources>
